The light microscopy images and hardness values of produced experimental steels with the chemical composition summarized in Table  1  are presented in Fig.  4 . During the tests, the rolling direction planes were observed. However, no banding was observed, which could indicate microsegregation in the sample. All three alloys exhibit similar microstructures. No visible and measurable differences in grain size, morphology or phase composition may be observed. The base steel is composed in majority of martensite; however, some areas reveal granular-like morphology, which may be classified as bainite. The steels alloyed with Mo and Cu are fully martensitic. The hardness results seem to confirm microscopic observations, as the base material has the lowest hardness of 416 HV1. The Mo alloyed steel has on average a 19 HV1 higher hardness. In case of the steel with additions of Mo and Cu, the average value was increased by 40 HV1. It is in agreement with the literature as both elements increase the precipitation hardening of steel [ 56 ]. Therefore, Cu–alloying provides an additional increase in hardness of 24 HV1, which corresponds to an additional strength of − 77 MPa, based on [ 57 ]. The solid solution strengthening of elements in ferrite is often estimated by linear equations, as for example, introduced by Gutiérrez et al. [ 58 ]. For the contribution of Cu it is most often referred to the data by Pickering et al. [ 58 ], which can be simplified to: 1 σ Cu = 39 M P a · c Cu \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
